WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
FedRAMP-oriented evidence work usually depends on several categories of documentation.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.500
System descriptions explain the purpose, architecture, users, data types, service model,

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.640
and operating context.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.460
Architecture diagrams show major components and relationships.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.600
Data flow descriptions explain how information moves through the service.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.940
Inventory records identify assets, components, dependencies, and ownership.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.280
These documents establish the factual base for assessment.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.980
Control implementation narratives connect security requirements to the actual service.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.280
Policies and procedures explain management expectations and repeatable processes.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.940
Configuration baselines describe expected secure states.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.680
Incident response plans explain how the organization prepares for, detects, escalates,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.100
responds to, and learns from security events.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.440
Contingency and recovery documentation explains resilience and restoration expectations.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:01.140
Vulnerability and change management procedures show how risk is handled over time.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.880
Operational records are just as important as formal documents.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.220
Change records, vulnerability tickets, access review artifacts, incident records,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.120
monitoring outputs, approval records, scan results,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.100
and continuous monitoring records show whether processes are being performed.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.240
A policy may say what should happen.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.020
Operational evidence shows what did happen.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.120
Both are needed because control expectations usually require design and operation.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.540
Different documents must tell a consistent story.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.720
The system description should match the architecture diagram.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.020
The diagram should match the inventory.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.280
The inventory should match scan scope and logging sources.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.380
Control narratives should match procedures and evidence.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.440
When documents contradict each other,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.500
reviewers have to resolve basic facts before they can evaluate risk.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.200
Consistency is one of the clearest signs that documentation is being maintained with the

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.460
real system.
